1.  1. What is firewalld
Dynamic firewall manager (used in RHEL / CentOS / Rocky / Alma / Fedora)
Uses zones instead of raw iptables
Changes can be runtime (temporary) or permanent

firewalld Important Concepts
-- Zone → trust level (public, private, internal, dmz, etc.)
-- Service → predefined port rules (ssh, http, https)
-- Port → manual rule (e.g., 8080/tcp)

Runtime vs Permanent
-- runtime → lost after reboot
-- permanent → saved config

firewalld Commands (Command → Action)
systemctl start firewalld                    # start firewall service
systemctl stop firewalld                     # stop firewall
systemctl restart firewalld                  # restart firewall
systemctl enable firewalld                   # enable on boot
systemctl disable firewalld                  # disable on boot
systemctl status firewalld                   # check service status

firewall-cmd --state                         # check if firewall is running
firewall-cmd --reload                        # reload rules (apply permanent changes)

firewall-cmd --get-zones                     # list all zones
firewall-cmd --get-active-zones              # show active zones
firewall-cmd --get-default-zone              # show default zone
firewall-cmd --set-default-zone=public       # set default zone

firewall-cmd --list-all                      # show full config (services, ports, interfaces)
firewall-cmd --list-services                 # list allowed services
firewall-cmd --list-ports                    # list open ports

firewall-cmd --add-service=ssh                # allow ssh (runtime)
firewall-cmd --add-service=http --permanent   # allow http permanently
firewall-cmd --remove-service=http --permanent   # remove http service

firewall-cmd --add-port=8080/tcp              # open port 8080 (temporary)
firewall-cmd --add-port=8080/tcp --permanent  # open port permanently
firewall-cmd --remove-port=8080/tcp --permanent   # close port

firewall-cmd --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="192.168.1.10" accept'   # allow specific IP
firewall-cmd --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="192.168.1.10" drop'     # block specific IP

firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-interface=eth0 --permanent   # bind interface to zone
firewall-cmd --get-services                                   # list predefined services
firewall-cmd --complete-reload                                # reset firewall completely
journalctl -u firewalld                                       # check firewall logs

